From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: libfstools: use an overlay in RAM when the overlay volume cannot be mounted

mount_root ignores the result of mount_overlay(), and mount_overlay() returned
the error when the volume could not be mounted. On the SR1010 a block of the
Plugin partition that JFFS2 cannot read (error -74, "cannot read OOB for EB")
made the mount fail with an I/O error, and the system stayed on the read-only
root file system: no overlay of any kind, so no host name, no network
configuration and no way to repair it without the serial console.

Fall back to the overlay in RAM, as is done when the switch to the overlay
fails, and log it.

--- a/libfstools/overlay.c
+++ b/libfstools/overlay.c
@@ -568,8 +568,17 @@
 	}
 
 	err = overlay_mount_fs(v, overlay_mp);
-	if (err)
-		return err;
+	if (err) {
+		/*
+		 * The volume cannot be mounted, for example JFFS2 stops at a block
+		 * it cannot read. Without an overlay the system would stay on the
+		 * read-only root file system, with no host name, no network
+		 * configuration and no way to repair it over the network. Use a
+		 * temporary overlay in RAM and say so.
+		 */
+		ULOG_ERR("cannot mount the overlay volume, using a temporary overlay in RAM: settings will not survive a reboot\n");
+		return ramoverlay();
+	}
 
 	/* The state and upper/work trees live here (a sub-directory if shared). */
 	fs_overlay_dir(overlay_mp, ovl_data, sizeof(ovl_data));
